  "constraints": {
    "no_return_promise": "Never promise or imply a return rate. The corpus number is arithmetic FROM a stated assumption, never a forecast. Say the assumption out loud in the VO the first time a number depends on it.",
    "withdrawal_rate_on_screen": "Every corpus figure must carry its assumed withdrawal/return rate ON SCREEN in the same frame as the number. A number without its assumption visible is a fabricated promise.",
    "no_unsourced_retire_early": "No 'retire early', 'quit your job', or financial-freedom-by-age claim that is not sourced and dated in facts-staging.md.",
    "market_rewrite_not_translation": "The cut is written for the US from scratch (US vehicles, US numbers, Brian voice) — never a translation. See vault/knowledge/us-english-script-style.md.",
    "derived_income_carries_assumption": "EXTENDED 2026-08-07 during ch2 review. `withdrawal_rate_on_screen` was written about CORPUS figures, so the build assert let s17's `WHAT ₹2,500 BUYS` through bare — correctly, by the letter. But ₹2,500/month is the corpus's OUTPUT at 3.0%, i.e. the promise the video actually makes, and `no_return_promise` says a number without its assumption visible is a fabricated promise. So a DERIVED INCOME figure carries the rate in frame or an explicit ILLUSTRATIVE marker, same as a corpus. Every rung has a 'what X buys' beat, so this lives in the build assert, not in per-chapter review."
  },
